Welcome back! In the last video, we saw visualizations of the cost function j, and how you can try

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:14.250
different choices if the parameter is w and b, and see what cost value that gets you.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:20.070
It would be nice if we had a more systematic way to find the values of w and b that result

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:26.430
in the smallest possible cost, j of w, b. It turns out there's an algorithm called

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:31.890
gradient descent that you can use to do that. Gradient descent is used all over the place

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:37.490
in machine learning, not just for linear regression, but for training, for example, some of the

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:44.430
most advanced neural network models, also called deep learning models. Deep learning models

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:48.030
are something you learn about in the second course.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:53.110
So learning this tool of gradient descent will set you up with one of the most important

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:57.870
building blocks in machine learning. Here's an overview of what we'll do with gradient

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:06.030
descent. You have the cost function j of w, b right here that you want to minimize. In

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:11.730
the example we've seen so far, this is a cost function for linear regression, but it

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:18.150
turns out that gradient descent is an algorithm that you can use to try to minimize any function,

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:24.990
not just a cost function for linear regression. Just to make this discussion on gradient descent

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:31.150
more general, it turns out that gradient descent applies to more general functions, including

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:37.910
other cost functions that work with models that have more than two parameters. So for

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:48.070
instance, if you have a cost function j as a function of w1, w2, up to wn, and b, your

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:57.150
objective is to minimize j over the parameters w1 to wn and b. In other words, you want to

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:06.610
pick values for w1 through wn and b that gives you the smallest possible value of j. It

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:11.570
turns out that gradient descent is an algorithm that you can apply to try to minimize this

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:19.290
cost function j as well. What you're going to do is just start off with some initial

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:25.410
guesses for w and b. In linear regression, it won't matter too much what the initial

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:32.370
values are, so a common choice is to set them both to 0. So for example, you can set w

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:38.290
to 0 and b to 0 as the initial guess. With the gradient descent algorithm, what you're

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:44.410
going to do is, you'll keep on changing the parameters w and b a bit every time to

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:53.750
try to reduce the cost j of wb until hopefully j settles at or near a minimum. One thing

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:59.370
I should note is that for some functions j that may not be a bow shape or a hammock

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:06.790
shape, it is possible for there to be more than one possible minimum. Let's take a look

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:13.790
at an example of a more complex surface plot j to see what gradient descent is doing. This

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:19.590
function is not a squared error cost function. For linear regression with the squared error

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:26.390
cost function, you always end up with a bow shape or a hammock shape. But this is the

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:31.710
type of cost function you might get if you're training a neural network model. Notice the

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:41.990
axis. That is w and b on the bottom axis. For different values of w and b, you get different

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:49.790
points on this surface j of wb where the height of the surface at some point is the value

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:56.190
of the cost function. Now, let's imagine that this surface plot is actually a view

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:02.110
of a slightly hilly outdoor park or a golf course where the high points are hills and

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:08.750
the low points are valleys like so. And I'd like you to imagine if you will that you're

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:15.110
physically standing at this point on the hill. And if it helps you to relax, imagine

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:20.150
that this loss of really nice green grass and butterflies and flowers is a really nice

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:29.190
hill and you go is to start up here and get to the bottom of one of these valleys as efficiently

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:38.550
as possible. So what the gradient descent algorithm does is you're going to spin around 360 degrees

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:45.350
and look around and ask yourself if I were to take a tiny little baby step in one direction

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:51.350
and I want to go downhill as quickly as possible toward one of these valleys, what direction

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:57.150
do I choose to take that baby step? Well, if you want to walk down this hill as efficiently

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.670
as possible, it turns out that if you're standing at this point in the hill and

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:06.750
you look around, you may notice that the best direction to take your next step downhill

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:13.590
is roughly that direction. Mathematically, this is the direction of steepest descent.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.670
And it means that when you take a tiny baby little step, this takes you downhill faster

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:24.550
than a tiny little baby step you could have taken in any other direction. So after taking

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:30.550
this first step, you're now at this point on the hill over here. Now let's repeat

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:37.590
the process. Standing at this new point, you're going to again spin around 360 degrees and

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:43.070
ask yourself in what direction would I take the next little baby step in order to move

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:50.550
downhill? And if you do that and take another step, you end up moving a bit in that direction

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:56.390
and you can keep going. From this new point, you can again look around and decide what

00:05:56.390 --> 00:06:03.310
direction would take you downhill most quickly. Take another step, another step, and so on,

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:09.630
until you find yourself at the bottom of this valley at this local minimum right here. What

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:14.270
you just did was go through multiple steps of gradient descent.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:21.590
It turns out gradient descent has an interesting property. Remember that you can choose a starting

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:27.550
point at the surface by choosing starting values for the parameters w and b. When you

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:34.430
perform gradient descent a moment ago, you had started at this point over here, right?

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:40.430
Now imagine if you try gradient descent again, but this time you choose a different starting

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:45.270
point by choosing parameters that place your starting point just a couple steps to the

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:52.470
right over here. If you then repeat the gradient descent process, which means you look around,

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:57.510
take a little step in the direction of the steepest descent, so you end up here, then

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:03.150
you again look around, take another step, and so on. And if you were to run gradient

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:08.590
descent the second time, starting just a couple steps to the right of where we did

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:15.510
the first time, then you will end up in a totally different valley, this different minimum

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:22.390
over here on the right. The bottoms of both the first and the second valleys are called

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:28.390
local minima, because if you start going down the first valley, gradient descent won't

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:33.510
lead you to the second valley, and the same is true if you started going down the

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:39.150
second valley, you would stay in that second minimum and not find your way into the first

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:45.510
local minimum. So this is an interesting property of the gradient descent algorithm,

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:51.230
and you'll see more about this later. So in this video, you saw how gradient descent

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:56.950
helps you go downhill. In the next video, let's look at the mathematical expressions

